Subject: JSON.stringify writes plain keys and primitive members of a cached shape straight from the slot, and never holds an unhashed shape

What this does
  - `JSONStringifyContext.walk_plain`: when a shape enters `walk_ok`, a bit per property (first 64) for keys that are 8-bit strings with nothing to escape
  - in the shape walk, with no replacer and no gap, a plain key whose value is a primitive in a plain data slot is written as `,"key":` with one room check and one copy, and the value straight from the slot, borrowed (`json_put_prim_borrowed`), with no reference count traffic
  - `json_str8`: str8 with the normal-string case inline (str8 itself was not inlined), used by the buffer writers
  - the shape walk is taken only for hashed shapes; an unhashed shape goes to the general key-list path

Why we need it
  After 0593 a third of stringify cycles went to per-key quoting and to dup/free of each member, and a call to str8 per string. json_app stringify only: -18.1% cycles, -17.0% instructions; whole json_app -2.9%.
  The walk (0585) held a reference to the object's shape for the whole walk; an unhashed shape is edited in place by add_property, which asserts it is unshared, so a getter adding a property to an object with an unhashed shape (for example after a delete) aborted (found by the new jkey.js test).

Soundness
  A hashed shape is never changed in place, and walk_ok holds it, so its key atoms and their strings stay alive and unchanged while walk_plain describes them. The fast write happens only while the object still has that shape and the property is a plain data property; nothing between reading the slot and writing it runs JS (buffer writes only), so the borrowed value stays alive. The bytes written are those json_quote_str and json_putc8 write for such a key. Gaps, replacers, non-plain keys, wide keys, accessors and properties past 64 take the existing code. Differentials identical old vs new: new jkey.js (36,079 outputs: 60 key sets of 1-90 keys with escaped, wide, surrogate-pair and 300-byte keys, all member types, accessors that add and delete properties mid-walk, non-enumerable members, 0-200 byte prefixes for buffer-room boundaries, gaps, replacers), and jstr, jprim, jedge, jdiff, jdiff2, jscan, jslot, jdt2, jdtoa.

